{
  "image_name": "13e68abb9227bd4663b44a9555908aa6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a spent lithium-ion battery graphite anode degrades and how its graphite can be recovered for reuse. At the top, the damaged anode is shown with typical failure features: decomposition of the solid-electrolyte interphase, lithium dendrites, dead lithium, an overly thick SEI layer, graphite cracking and exfoliation, and corrosion or cracking of the copper current collector. Together, these defects explain why the anode becomes inactive and needs regeneration.\n\nBelow, four recycling or regeneration routes are compared. One uses froth flotation after binder removal, with chemical treatment or pyrolysis helping separate graphite from impurities. Another uses thermal regeneration, involving leaching, physical separation, and calcination. A third route is electrochemical regeneration, where the spent anode and a graphite plate are placed in electrolyte so ions and contaminants can be removed or redistributed from the graphite-coated copper foil. The last route uses ultrasonication for binder removal followed by microwave treatment, apparently producing surface micro-plasma effects.\n\nAll routes converge on regenerated graphite. On the right, the figure highlights its possible applications: direct reuse in lithium-ion batteries as a primary route, or secondary uses such as polymer composites, water filtration, gas storage, and supercapacitors.",
